A copy of all these rules and regulations are attached to this chapter.
(Ord. of 12-13-2005, § I; Ord. of 4-7-2016; Res. of 3-6-2025, § 1)

556:
(a) Before any manufactured home or mobile home is located or relocated on any site in Brantley County, the owner shall first apply to building officials for a permit to locate or relocated such manufactured home or mobile home in Brantley County,
(b) The application for a permit shall include the following information:
(1) Name of the owner;
(2) Name of the occupant, if different from the owner;
(3) The location of the manufactured home or mobile home at the time the application is being made;
(4) The name of the owner of the real estate on which the manufactured home or mobile home is to be situated identified by tax map and parcel number;
(5) If the owner is an installer or manufactured home dealer, the current location of the manufacture home or mobile home and destination where the home is to be relocated identified by a map and parcel; and
(6) If the owner is an installer or manufactured home dealer, the application shall include his or her respective license numbers as issued by the State of Georgia;
(7) Proof that the manufactured home or mobile home has permanently affixed a certificate or tag that it was constructed in accordance with the Act, or the preceding act.
(c) The permit fee for a manufactured home or a mobile home within the county, new or used is at $300.00.
(d) The permit fee for a manufactured home or a mobile home coming into the county from outside the county, new or used is at $600.00.
(Ord. of 6-8-2010, § 3; Amend. of 9-9-2021)

583:
(a) Definition. Conduct which shall be deemed to be offensive and adverse to public safety and thereby classified as disorderly conduct is exhibited or occurs when:
(1) Any person acts in a violent or tumultuous manner towards another whereby any person is placed in fear of safety of his life, limb or health;
(2) Any person acts in a violent or tumultuous manner towards another whereby the property of any person is placed in danger of being destroyed or damaged;
(3) Any person endangers the lawful pursuits of another by acts of violence, angry threats and abusive language;
(4) Any person causes, provokes or engages in any fight, brawl or riotous acts which endanger the life, limb, health or property of another;
(5) Any person utters, while in a state of anger in a public place, any bawdy, lewd, obscene or fighting words or epithets directed toward another person to demean or provoke such person; or
(6) Any person congregates with another or others in or on any public way so as to halt the flow of vehicular or pedestrian traffic and refuses to clear such public way when so ordered by a lawful authority.
(b) Prohibited. No person shall engage in or exhibit disorderly conduct within the unincorporated areas of the county.
(Ord. of 5-9-1995, §§ 2, 3)

598:
(a) Any person violating this article, or any provision, shall, upon conviction, be punished as follows:
(1) First offense, by a warning with a fine of $10.00 which is to be suspended unless a second violation occurs within a period of 30 days from the entry of sentence;
(2) Second offense, by a fine of $25.00;
(3) Third offense, by a fine of $50.00;
(4) Fourth offense, by a fine of $100.00;
(5) Fifth and all subsequent offenses, by a fine of not less than $250.00 and not more than $1,000.00, and/or a sentence of one to 60 days in the county jail. The magistrate court shall have authority to permit the sentenced party to perform community service in lieu of payment of that portion of a fine imposed above $250.00 at the rate of $6.00 per hour; however, this would not include any statutory imposed fine add-ons. The performance of such community service shall be in compliance with such conditions as the magistrate may order.
(b) No person accused of violating this article shall be arrested prior to trial, but any person who fails to appear for trial shall be arrested thereafter on the warrant of the magistrate, and required to post bond for his future appearance.
(c) Violations of this article shall be tried upon citations which shall be issued to an alleged violator of any provisions of this article. Such citation shall state the time and place at which the accused is to appear for trial, shall identify the offense with which the accused is charged, shall have an identifying number by which it shall be filed with the court, shall indicate the identity of the accused and the date of service, and shall be signed by the representative of the county who completes and serves it. The citation may be served by certified mail, return receipt requested.
(d) Citations issued under this article may be tried without a prosecuting attorney; however, if the prosecuting attorney is needed, the county attorney shall serve as the prosecuting attorney.
(e) Violations of this chapter shall be tried in the magistrate court of the county, and shall be tried in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 15-10-60 et seq.
(f) Nothing in this article shall prevent the board of commissioners from bringing any civil action for injunction, or other proceedings to abate any violation of this article or other state or federal law. No sanction, penalty or remedy prescribed in this section shall be considered exclusive of any other remedy, but shall be available in addition to any other sanctions, penalty or remedy provided by law.
(g) Each violation of this article shall constitute a separate offense.
(Ord. of 3-13-2001, § 3)

603:
The following words, terms and phrases when used in this article shall have the meaning ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Board of commissioners shall mean the duly elected board of commissioners;
Brantley County shall mean the unincorporated area of Brantley County, a political subdivision of the State of Georgia;
Hour shall refer to Eastern Standard Time zone or daylight savings time, whichever is appropriate for days, months, of the year.
Neighboring inhabitant shall mean people who live within or occupy a residence within 1,000 feet from the building, structure, vehicle or site in which the noise emanates; and
Noise shall mean the type of noises delineated in section 54-114 of this article;
Person shall mean an individual, corporation, partnership, association or other legal entity but does not include the State of Georgia or any political subdivision thereof.
(Ord. of 12-11-2001, § 3; Ord. of 8-10-2010, § 2)
Editor's note— Section 2 of an ordinance adopted Aug. 10, 2010, changed the title of § 54-113 from "Definitions of loud, offensive and disturbing noises" to "Definitions."

605:
The standards which shall be considered in determining whether a violation of this article exists shall include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) The volume of noise;
(2) The intensity of the noise;
(3) Whether the nature of the noise is usual or unusual;
(4) Whether the origin of the noise is natural or unnatural;
(5) The volume and intensity of the background noise, if any;
(6) The proximity of the noise to residential sleeping facilities;
(7) The nature of the area within which the noise emanates;
(8) The density of inhabitation of the area within which the noise emanates;
(9) The time of day or night the noise occurs;
(10) The duration of the noise;
(11) Whether the noise is recurrent, intermittent or constant; and
(12) Whether the noise is produced by a commercial or noncommercial activity.
(Ord. of 8-10-2010, § 3)
Editor's note— See editor's note to § 54-114.

692:
No person shall:
(1) Deposit, place or leave any items other than garbage in any receptacle (dumpster) provided by the county for the collection of garbage;
(2) Dump, deposit, throw, leave or cause the dumping, depositing, throwing, placing or leaving of garbage or litter in the immediate area of any garbage collection receptacle;
(3) Place, throw or deposit any animal carcass, or any live animal in any garbage collection receptacle;
(4) Place, throw or deposit any burning or smoldering material in any garbage collection receptacle;
(5) Set fire to the contents of any garbage collection receptacle;
(6) Deposit, place or leave any explosive or spontaneous combustible materials in any garbage collection receptacle;
(7) Sort through, scatter or remove any of the garbage which has been placed and left in a garbage collection receptacle. This subsection shall not apply to any county employee or agent who is in the performance of his official duties;
(8) Enter, climb in or on, or go into any garbage collection receptacles. This subsection shall not apply to any county employee or agent who is in the performance of his official duties; or
(9) Deposit, place or leave any items in a garbage collection receptacle unless that party is a resident of the county. However, this subsection shall not apply to a nonresident who is traveling through the county, and who has not entered the county for the purpose of disposing of garbage.
(10) Place, deposit, or leave or cause the placing, depositing or leaving of any motor vehicle tire in any garbage collection receptacle, or in the immediate area of any garbage collection receptacle.
(Res. of 5-15-1991, § 3; Ord. of 3-12-2002, § 1)

703:
(a) Pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 48-5-242, the board of commissioners may waive in whole or in part, the collection of any amount due to Brantley County, for penalties or interest assessed for failure to comply with the law governing assessment and collection of ad valorem taxes. The tax commissioner shall determine whether or not the default giving rise to the penalty or interest was due to a reasonable cause and not due to gross or willful neglect of the law or of regulations or instructions issued pursuant to law. The tax commissioner may recommend to the board of commissioners the waiver of penalties and interest, in whole or in part.
(b) The waiver of penalties or interest in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 48-5-242 and under the provision of this section shall be subject to the following conditions:
(1) Only interest that accrues on or after July 1, 2002 may be waived;
(2) The taxpayer shall provide a claim in writing, under oath, stating the specific reason for the taxpayer's failure to pay the ad valorem taxes in a timely manner;
(3) The tax commissioner advises the board of commissioners that the default giving rise to penalty or interest was not a result of:
a. Gross or willful negligence;
b. A disregard of law or regulations or instructions issued pursuant to law;
c. That the taxpayer has not been delinquent in the payment of other taxes owed to Brantley County or other counties; and
d. Taxes and penalties will impose a substantial hardship upon the taxpayer.
(c) The board of commissioners will make the final decision as to whether to waive the penalties and interest in whole or in part.
(Ord. of 9-8-2009, §§ 1—3)
Editor's note— An ordinance adopted Sept. 8, 2009, §§ 1—3, did not specify manner of inclusion; hence, codification as § 74-1 was at the discretion of the editor.
